const fitSections = [
  {
    category: "Skechers is not one brand, it's a dozen lasts",
    text: [
      `Every shoe is built around a <strong>last</strong>: a rigid foot-shaped form that determines length, toe box volume, instep height, and heel width. The last is to footwear what a fit block is to clothing: the base geometry everything else inherits. We covered how that works for garments in <a href="/blog/why-the-same-size-fits-differently/" class="${linkClass}">why the same size fits differently across brands</a>; footwear is the same story with harder edges, because a shoe can't drape over a mismatch the way a t-shirt can.`,
      `Most footwear brands run a small number of lasts and keep them stable for years. Skechers is unusual in how wide its range is: walking lines, structured-support lines, slip-on systems, chunky retro silhouettes, and a full occupational range all coexist, and they are not built on the same form. A shoe designed to be pulled on without hands has to grip the heel differently than one with six eyelets of lacing. A shoe with a molded support footbed has less internal volume than one with a flat foam insert.`,
      `So the brand-level advice you'll read everywhere ("Skechers runs true to size") is true on average and unhelpful in practice. Averages don't ship; individual models do. Treat your size as a property of the line, not of the brand, and expect to learn two or three of them rather than one.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    category: 'Width is the variable most people get wrong',
    text: [
      `Here is the single most useful thing to know about buying Skechers, and the reason the brand is worth the trouble for a lot of feet: they actually stock widths, across a large part of the range, in men's and women's, which most mainstream sneaker brands do not.`,
      `US footwear expresses width as a letter, and the same letter means different things by gender. Skechers publishes its own fit ladder, and it has three rungs rather than the two most shoppers know about:`,
      {
        minWidth: 640,
        headers: ['Fit', "Men's width", "Women's width", 'Extra room vs. medium', 'Where the room is'],
        rows: [
          ['Relaxed Fit®', 'D', 'B', 'None — medium width', 'Toe box only'],
          ['Wide Fit', '2E', 'D', `Men's ~5/8", Women's ~1/2"`, 'Throughout the shoe'],
          ['Extra Wide Fit', '4E', 'not offered', `Men's ~1"`, 'Throughout the shoe'],
        ],
      },
      `The row that surprises people is the first one. Relaxed Fit is not a wide fitting. It's a standard medium width, D for men and B for women, with extra space in the toe box only, holding a snug heel. If you have been buying Relaxed Fit because your feet are wide, you have been buying a medium with a roomier front, and the sides of your forefoot have been getting no extra room at all. Skechers' own framing is that Relaxed Fit suits people who want space up front but a secure heel; if you need room through the shoe, it isn't the answer.`,
      `Wide Fit is where actual width begins: men's 2E, women's D, adding roughly five-eighths of an inch for men and half an inch for women. Extra Wide takes men to 4E and about a full inch, and per Skechers' current guidance there is no women's Extra Wide Fit at all, which matters if you have been searching for one and assuming you missed it.`,
      `One more inconsistency worth knowing, in the same spirit as the conflicting conversion charts above. The footnote printed beneath Skechers' women's size chart says styles marked EWW belong to the Extra Wide Fit Collection, on a chart for a category their own fit guide says has no Extra Wide option. And the footnote beneath the men's chart puts Wide Fit at "2E / 3E," while the fit guide puts Wide at 2E and reserves 4E for Extra Wide, with no 3E anywhere. Both notices can't be current. Where the product page and the fit guide disagree, buy from the width actually offered in the size selector on the shoe you want.`,
      `Now the part that matters. <strong>When a shoe pinches across the ball of the foot, most people size up — and sizing up is the wrong fix.</strong> Going up a length adds forefoot width slowly and adds heel-to-toe length quickly, so you end up with a shoe that still presses at the widest point while your heel slips and your toes slide forward on every step. The symptoms then look like a cushioning or lacing problem, and no amount of insole swapping solves them.`,
      `If your feet are wide, ordering your true length in a wide fitting will almost always beat ordering a half or full size up in a medium. If they're narrow, the reverse trap applies: a medium in a roomy line can leave you cinching the laces hard enough to fold the upper, which is a sign you want a snugger platform rather than a smaller size.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    category: 'Arch Fit changes the fit, not just the feel',
    text: [
      `Arch Fit is easy to read as a comfort upgrade: better insole, same shoe. It isn't. The structured, contoured footbed occupies real volume inside the shoe and holds the foot in a more fixed position, so the same nominal size has less room to give than a flat-foam equivalent and distributes pressure differently across the instep.`,
      `Two practical consequences. First, if you're moving to Arch Fit from another Skechers line, don't assume your size transfers untested; the length may be right while the volume is not. Second, on models where the footbed is removable, taking it out is a genuine fit adjustment rather than just a comfort preference. It's worth trying before you conclude the size is wrong, and worth knowing if you intend to use a custom orthotic, which needs that space back.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    category: 'Slip-ins trade adjustability for a fixed heel',
    text: [
      `The Hands Free Slip-ins system works by building a supportive structure at the heel that holds your foot in place as you step in, rather than relying on you to tighten anything. It's genuinely clever, and it changes the sizing maths in a way almost nobody writes down: <strong>there is no lacing to compensate with.</strong>`,
      `In a lace-up, a shoe that's a touch long is recoverable: you snug it down and the heel stays put. In a slip-in, the heel is held by geometry that assumes your foot is where it should be. Slightly too large and it will feel loose no matter what you do; slightly too small and the fixed heel structure has nowhere to yield. The effective tolerance is narrower on both sides, so this is the line where getting the size right the first time matters most, and the line where free returns are worth using deliberately rather than guessing.`,
    ],
  },
];

