const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Why does Skechers publish conflicting UK and EU conversions?',
    a: "Because it publishes more than one chart. Open the size modal on a unisex style, a women's style, and a men's style and you will get three different tables, and they do not agree with each other. On the unisex chart, a women's 9.5 is listed as UK 7 / EU 41; on the women's chart, a women's 9.5 is UK 6.5 / EU 39.5. That's a full size and a half of disagreement in the EU column, published by the same brand on the same website. Use the chart on the product page you are actually buying from, and convert from your centimetre measurement rather than from a UK or EU size you think you are.",
  },
  {
    q: 'For a wide foot, should I size up or order a wide fitting?',
    a: 'Order the wide fitting in your true length. When a shoe pinches across the ball of the foot, most people size up. Sizing up is the wrong fix. Going up a length adds forefoot width slowly and adds heel-to-toe length quickly, so you end up with a shoe that still presses at the widest point while your heel slips and your toes slide forward on every step. This single correction fixes more Skechers returns than anything else.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What does Skechers Wide equal in letter widths?',
    a: "Skechers' fit guide puts Wide Fit at 2E for men and D for women, with Extra Wide at 4E for men only. Note that the footnotes printed beneath their size charts say something different — men's Wide as '2E / 3E', and an EWW Extra Wide designation on the women's chart, which their fit guide says doesn't exist. Where the two disagree, go by the widths actually offered in the size selector on the shoe you're buying.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Does Arch Fit change my size?',
    a: "It can. The structured, contoured footbed occupies real volume inside the shoe and holds the foot in a more fixed position, so the same nominal size has less room to give than a flat-foam equivalent. If you're moving to Arch Fit from another Skechers line, don't assume your size transfers untested; the length may be right while the volume is not. On models where the footbed is removable, taking it out is a genuine fit adjustment rather than just a comfort preference.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Does Skechers run small?',
    a: "The brand-level advice you'll read everywhere (“Skechers runs true to size”) is true on average and unhelpful in practice. Averages don't ship; individual models do. Skechers doesn't sell one fit; it sells a catalogue of fit platforms that happen to share a logo, and each one is built on a different foot-shaped form with a different amount of room inside it. Treat your size as a property of the line, not of the brand.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Skechers Relaxed Fit the same as Wide Fit?',
    a: "No. Relaxed Fit is a standard medium width, D for men and B for women, with extra room in the toe box only, keeping a snug heel. Wide Fit is genuinely wider throughout the shoe: men's 2E, women's D. If your foot is wide along the sides, Relaxed Fit won't solve it; Wide Fit is the one that adds width where you need it.",
  },
