---

<BrandGuide
  brandKey="skechers"
  brand="Skechers"
  title="Skechers Size Guide: Why Your Size Changes Between Arch Fit, Slip-ins, and Go Walk"
  description="Skechers isn't one fit. Lasts, widths, closure systems, and memory foam decide your size more than the number on the box. Full charts and a decision flow."
  h1="Skechers Size Chart & Fit Guide: Why Your Size Changes Between Lines"
  chartsHeading="The charts"
  standardCharts={false}
  typicalCharts={charts}
  fitSections={fitSections}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={related}
>
  <Fragment slot="verdict">
    <p>
      Do these in sequence. Most sizing mistakes come from skipping to step four:
    </p>
    <ol class="mt-3 list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
      <li>
        <strong>Get your length in centimetres, not your size.</strong> A size is a claim about your
        foot; a measurement is your foot. Everything downstream depends on this number being right.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Read length off the chart, then stop thinking about length.</strong> Resist the urge
        to adjust for anything yet.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Choose width deliberately.</strong> If shoes routinely feel tight across the ball of
        your foot while your heel slips, you are a wide fitting in your true length, not a medium in
        a bigger size. This single correction fixes more Skechers returns than anything else.
        Skechers confirms this directly: switching from Medium to Wide or Extra Wide does not change
        your length. A size 9 medium is a size 9 wide.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Adjust for the platform, not the brand.</strong> Structured support footbeds take up
        volume. Slip-ins have no lacing to compensate with, so aim for exact. Soft-foam walking
        models will loosen slightly as they compress. Retro silhouettes run generous.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Learn your size in a line once and reuse it.</strong> A brand's individual lines are
        far more consistent with themselves than with each other. One successful purchase in Arch
        Fit teaches you Arch Fit — it doesn't teach you Go Walk.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3">
      The number on the box was never the specification. The last it was built on, the width it was
      cut for, and the foam it shipped with are the specification, and unlike the number, all three
      are things you can actually check before you buy.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="charts-intro">
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      A warning before the numbers, and it's the most useful thing on this page.
      <strong>Skechers does not publish one conversion chart.</strong> Open the size modal on a unisex
      style, a women's style, and a men's style and you will get three different tables — and they do
      not agree with each other. On the unisex chart, a women's 9.5 is listed as UK 7 / EU 41. On the
      women's chart, a women's 9.5 is UK 6.5 / EU 39.5. That's a full size and a half of disagreement
      in the EU column, published by the same brand on the same website.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      The practical consequence: <strong
        >use <AffLink brand="skechers"
          >the chart on the product page you are actually buying from</AffLink
        ></strong
      >, and convert from your centimetre measurement rather than from a UK or EU size you think you
      are. The centimetre column is the only one that means the same thing everywhere.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="charts-outro">
    <p class="mt-6 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      One more caveat straight from Skechers: <strong
        >performance running and walking footwear sizes may vary</strong
      >, which is the brand's own acknowledgement that these tables are a starting point rather than
      a guarantee.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      Measure late in the day, standing, with the socks you'll actually wear, and use the longer
      foot (most people have one). Our <a
        href="/how-to-measure/"
        class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum"
        >measuring guide</a
      > covers the method in a few minutes.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="tips">
    <li>
      <strong>Memory foam compresses, so month-one fit isn't month-six fit:</strong> Skechers built
      much of its reputation on soft foam cushioning, and soft foam does what soft foam does: it
      packs down. Over weeks of wear, the insole and midsole compress and take a set, which slightly
      increases the internal volume of the shoe and lowers your foot within it.
    </li>
    <li>
      This is the mechanism behind a contradiction you'll see all over the reviews for the same
      model: one buyer reports it snug, another reports it roomy. Both are often describing the same shoe
      at different points in its life. A pair that fits <em>perfectly</em> out of the box, with no room
      anywhere, will likely feel a half-step loose once it's broken in; a pair that feels faintly firm
      underfoot on day one may be closer to correct than it seems. And when you replace a well-loved
      pair with the identical model, order your measured size rather than the size that pair <em
        >feels</em
      > like now, because you're comparing new against compressed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Occupational and safety footwear.</strong> Protective toe caps (steel, alloy, or
      composite) are rigid inserts that fix the shape and height of the toe box. They don't flex or
      break in, so toe clearance you'd get away with in a sneaker becomes a daily problem here. Order
      with more forefoot room than you would in a walking shoe, and pay attention to width, since a
      rigid cap can't accommodate a wide foot the way a knit upper will.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Kids' sizes.</strong> Children's footwear is segmented into ranges (toddler, little
      kid, big kid) whose numbering restarts, so the same printed number appears in more than one
      range and means different lengths. Buy by the range and the measured foot length, never by the
      number alone.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Chunky retro silhouettes.</strong> The heavier lifestyle models are built on generous
      lasts with substantial uppers, and tend to feel roomier than the brand's walking lines at the
      same size.
    </li>
  </Fragment>
</BrandGuide>
